<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<class name="CharacterMovementComponent2D" inherits="Node" api_type="extension" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/godotengine/godot/master/doc/class.xsd">
	<brief_description>
		Walking, falling and jumping for a 2D pawn.
	</brief_description>
	<description>
		The 3D component's twin, and deliberately a twin rather than a shared base: Godot keeps 2D and 3D in separate hierarchies all the way down, and GFGD already follows that with [PlayerStart2D] and [PlayerStart3D].
		What [i]is[/i] shared is everything with no dimension in it. [MovementState], [MovementInput], [ProposedMove], [FloorResult] and [MovementMode] are the same classes as in 3D; a 2D state keeps its position in the x and y of a [Vector3] and leaves z alone. That way there is one set of types, one mode contract and one prediction story rather than two.
		[b]Units are pixels[/b], and the defaults are Unreal's centimetre values unchanged - which is what the usual "a metre is a hundred pixels" convention makes them. The 3D component divides the same numbers by a hundred.
		[b]Up is negative Y.[/b] Godot's 2D screen axis points down, so [member up_direction] defaults to [code](0, -1)[/code].
		Not in the 2D component yet: crouching, swimming, flying, moving platforms, layered moves and transitions. Those are 3D only for now.
	</description>
